insert into public.articles (slug, title, excerpt, body_md, tags, min_level, published_at)
values
  ('when-you-cannot-stop-checking-your-phone', 'When You Cannot Stop Checking Your Phone', 'You pick it up to check one thing and surface forty minutes later. The pull is not a willpower failure. What the checking is actually doing for you, and how to loosen its grip.', 'You put the phone down. Within a minute your hand goes back to it, without a decision being made anywhere you can see. You unlock it to check the weather and come back to yourself much later, having read nothing you will remember, feeling slightly worse than before.

This happens to nearly everyone now, and most people treat it as a personal failing. It is not. It is what happens when a very ordinary brain meets something engineered to be checked. But it is also not hopeless, and the way out is not throwing your phone in a drawer forever.

What the checking is really doing

Notice what happens right before you reach for it. Usually it is a small gap: a moment of boredom, an awkward pause, a hard task you do not want to start, a feeling you do not want to sit with. The phone fills the gap instantly and reliably, which is exactly why the habit is so strong.

So the checking is rarely about the phone. It is about avoiding a few seconds of discomfort. Once you see that, the question changes from how do I use my phone less to what am I trying not to feel.

The other thing it does is deliver the occasional good thing: a message from someone you like, something funny, a piece of news that matters. Most checks give you nothing. A few give you something. That unpredictable pattern is the most powerful way to build a habit that exists, which is why it feels so much like a pull rather than a choice.

Start by watching, not stopping

Before you change anything, spend two or three days simply noticing. When you reach for the phone, ask: what just happened? Bored. Anxious about the email. Did not know what to do with my hands. Wanting to avoid this conversation.

You do not need to stop yourself. Just name it. Most people find two or three situations account for most of their checking, and knowing them is more useful than any app limit.

Put friction in the way

Willpower is a weak tool against a habit this practiced. Friction is stronger. Move the apps that pull you off your home screen, into a folder, or off the phone entirely so you have to use the browser. Turn off every notification that is not a person contacting you directly. Turn the screen to gray, which takes away much of the pull without taking away function.

The goal is not to make it impossible. It is to add two seconds of effort, which is often enough for the reaching hand to be interrupted by an actual decision.

Give your hands and your gaps something else

A habit does not disappear; it gets replaced. Decide in advance what fills the gaps. A book left on the arm of the chair. A crossword. A notebook. A window to look out of. Standing up and stretching. Even doing nothing counts, but it helps to have decided that doing nothing is the plan, rather than discovering it as an absence.

Make some places phone-free

Blanket rules are hard. Place-based rules are much easier to keep, because you are not deciding each time. Not at the table. Not in the bedroom. Not on the walk. Not in the first hour of the morning. Pick one, keep it for a week, and see how it feels before adding another.

The bedroom one is worth trying first for most people. A cheap alarm clock and a charger in another room changes both the last thirty minutes of the day and the first thirty, and that alone shifts how the whole day feels.

Notice how you feel afterward

Not while you are scrolling. Afterward. Most people, honestly checked, feel a bit flat, a bit behind, a bit more anxious than before they picked it up. Some things on the phone leave you better: a real conversation, something you actually wanted to learn, a photo from someone you love. Learning to tell those apart from the rest gives you a reason to change that does not depend on shame.

Watch for the news and comparison loops

Two patterns deserve special attention. Constant news checking, especially of worrying news, feeds anxiety while feeling like responsibility; you are not better informed at the twentieth check than the second. And scrolling through other people''s edited lives reliably makes people feel worse about their own. If either is a big part of your checking, address it directly: fixed times for news, and a ruthless unfollowing of accounts that leave you feeling small.

Expect to slip

Some days you will lose an evening to it. That is not proof that nothing works. It usually means the day was hard and the gaps were wider. Notice it, be decent to yourself about it, and go back to the small rules tomorrow. The aim is a general direction, not a perfect record.

When it is more than a habit

If the checking is keeping you up at night, hurting your work or your relationships, or if you feel real distress when you cannot check, it is worth taking seriously. Compulsive checking often sits on top of anxiety, low mood, or loneliness, and those are what actually need attention. A therapist can help you find what the phone is holding at bay, and the habit gets much easier to change once that is being looked after. The goal was never a phone-free life. It was getting your attention back for the things you would rather spend it on.', array['Habits', 'Worry']::text[], 0, '2026-09-20T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('the-quiet-grief-of-infertility', 'The Quiet Grief of Infertility', 'Every month is a small loss, and almost nobody around you sees it. Why trying to conceive can be one of the loneliest kinds of grief, and what helps when hope keeps having to restart.', 'Another month. The test, the waiting, the answer you did not want. And then, somehow, work in the morning, and small talk, and someone''s pregnancy announcement in your feed before you have had coffee.

Infertility is a grief that most people carry in private. There is no funeral, no casserole, no one asking how you are holding up, because most of the people in your life do not know. And unlike most losses, it does not happen once. It happens again and again, and each time you have to rebuild hope in order to try, which means each time there is something new to lose.

Name it as grief

A lot of the pain gets worse because people tell themselves they have no right to it. Nothing happened. Nobody died. But something did happen. You are losing a future you had pictured, over and over. You may be grieving the pregnancy you imagined, the child you had already named in your head, the version of your life that was supposed to be happening by now.

That is real loss, and you are allowed to grieve it without apology or comparison.

The body makes it harder

Infertility is one of the few griefs that lives in your own body and often in a medical process. Appointments, injections, blood draws, timed intimacy, waiting rooms. Your body becomes a project with a monthly verdict, and it is easy to start feeling like it is failing you or that you are failing at something everyone else does by accident.

You are not failing. Bodies are complicated, and the reasons are usually far outside anyone''s control or effort. This is not a test of how much you want it.

What it does to a couple

If you have a partner, this will press on the relationship. You may grieve at different speeds or in different ways: one wanting to talk about it constantly, the other wanting to think about anything else. One may be ready to stop or to try a different path while the other is not. Intimacy that has become scheduled and clinical can be hard to find your way back from.

Say these things out loud rather than assuming. I need to talk about it tonight. I need one evening where we do not. I am somewhere different from you on this and I do not want us to pull apart over it. Protect some time together that has nothing to do with trying.

Handling other people

Well-meaning people will say painful things. Just relax and it will happen. Have you tried this. At least you can travel. My cousin got pregnant the moment they stopped trying. Most of it comes from discomfort, not cruelty, and none of it needs to be absorbed as truth.

You are allowed to tell people what you need: I am not up for questions about this. Please do not give advice. I love you and I cannot come to the baby shower. You are also allowed to mute pregnancy announcements, skip the gathering, and protect yourself from the parts of the world that are hardest right now. That is not bitterness. It is care.

Let yourself have the bad days

Some days you will be functional and some days a stranger''s stroller will undo you. You can be genuinely happy for a friend and devastated at the same time; both are true and neither cancels the other. Let the hard days be hard rather than adding guilt on top.

Find people who understand

This is a grief that eases with company. A friend who has been through it. A support group, in person or online, where nobody needs the background explained. A counselor who works with fertility. Being around people who understand the vocabulary and the calendar is a relief that is hard to describe until you have it.

Make room for questions about what comes next

At some point, many people face decisions about how long to keep trying, whether to pursue treatment or a different path to parenthood, or whether to build a life that does not include children. These are enormous questions and they do not have to be answered this month. When you do get there, it helps to talk them through with someone outside the situation, and to notice that choosing to stop is a decision made out of self-respect rather than defeat.

If it gets very dark

Month after month of loss can wear a person down badly. If you find yourself hopeless, unable to function, or having thoughts of harming yourself, please reach out today. In the US you can call or text 988 at any time.

When to get more support

If sadness is with you most days, if you have withdrawn from people you love, if anxiety is running your life, or if the treatment process has left you feeling numb and mechanical, please talk to a therapist, ideally one who works with fertility and loss. This is one of the hardest things a person can go through quietly, and you should not have to do it alone. Whatever happens with your family, your grief deserves care of its own.', array['Grief', 'Loss']::text[], 0, '2026-09-20T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('the-anger-under-your-anxiety', 'The Anger Under Your Anxiety', 'You worry constantly and rarely get angry. For some people those two facts are connected. How anger that has nowhere to go can come back as worry, and what to do when you find it.', 'You are an anxious person. You worry, you overthink, you feel wound tight most of the time. What you almost never feel is angry. If asked, you would say you are not really an angry person. Other people in your life get angry; you get worried.

For some people, those two things turn out to be the same thing wearing different clothes. Not for everyone, and anxiety has many roots. But if the description above fits you closely, it is worth looking at.

Why anger can turn into worry

Anger is a signal. It tends to arrive when something is unfair, when a line has been crossed, when you need something you are not getting. It is meant to move you: to speak up, to push back, to change something.

But a lot of people learned early that anger was not safe. Maybe someone in your house had a temper that frightened everyone, so you decided never to be like that. Maybe anger in you was punished, or met with withdrawal, or treated as unacceptable in a way it was not for others. Maybe your job in the family was to keep things calm.

When anger has nowhere to go, it does not vanish. It gets converted into something more permitted. For many people that something is anxiety: a restless, vigilant, uncomfortable state that feels safer than the feeling underneath, because it points at the future rather than at a person.

How to tell whether this is you

A few signs. You worry a great deal but almost never feel angry. You find yourself anxious specifically after interactions where you were treated badly and said nothing. You have physical tension, jaw or shoulders or stomach, without a clear worry attached. You get irritated at small, safe things, the traffic, the dishes, while remaining unbothered by large unfair ones. You feel guilty at even the first flicker of anger, and it disappears quickly into worry about whether you are a bad person.

None of these is proof. Together, they are a pattern worth exploring.

Look at what happens before the spike

For the next while, when anxiety jumps, ask a different question than usual. Instead of what am I worried about, ask what just happened, and was there anything in it that was not okay?

Often you will find something: a comment that stung, a request you did not want to agree to, being talked over, taking on someone else''s job again. The anxiety arrived right after, dressed as worry about something unrelated.

Let yourself say the angry sentence, privately

You do not have to express anger at anyone to feel it. Try writing it down, in the bluntest words you have. That was not fair. I did not want to do that. I am furious that this keeps landing on me. Nobody sees it. You are not doing anything to anyone. You are just letting the signal exist long enough to be read.

Many people find that the anxiety drops noticeably once the real feeling has been named, even with no action taken.

Move it through the body

Anger is a physical state, and one that has been held down for years tends to be stored physically. A hard walk, pushing against a wall, squeezing and releasing your fists, a loud song in the car. Not to work yourself up, but to let the charge move rather than sit. Many people feel calmer afterward in a way that talking has not achieved.

Then ask what it was pointing at

Anger is information. Once you can feel it, ask what it wants. Usually it names something concrete: I need to say no to this. I need to tell them how that landed. I need to stop being the one who always handles this. That is the useful end of anger, and it is the part that avoidance costs you.

Acting on it does not mean shouting. It usually means one calm sentence you have been swallowing for years.

Expect it to feel wrong

If you grew up believing anger was dangerous, the first times you let yourself feel it will be uncomfortable. You may feel guilty, frightened, or convinced you are turning into the person you did not want to be. Notice that feeling angry and behaving badly are two different things, and that you have spent a lifetime proving you can hold the second. You are allowed to have the feeling.

When to get help with it

If anxiety is running your days, if you cannot locate anger at all even when something is clearly unfair, or if looking in this direction brings up old memories that feel too big to handle alone, please talk to a therapist. This pattern usually starts young and in a specific place, and it untangles much more easily with someone alongside you. Anxiety that has been carrying anger for decades can ease a great deal once the anger is allowed to be what it is.', array['Anger', 'Anxiety']::text[], 0, '2026-09-20T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('making-a-decision-you-cannot-undo', 'Making a Decision You Cannot Undo', 'Some choices do not come with a way back: a surgery, a move, leaving a marriage, having a child. How to think clearly when the stakes are permanent and the fear is loud.', 'Most decisions are reversible. You can quit the job, return the sofa, change your mind about the class. A few are not. Whether to have surgery. Whether to end a marriage. Whether to have a child, or another one. Whether to move across the world. Whether to stop treatment.

These decisions feel different, and they should. The usual advice about just picking something and adjusting later does not apply. Here is a way to think about them that does.

Slow down without stalling

Irreversible decisions deserve time. They do not deserve infinite time, and the difference matters. Deciding takes energy and holds your life in suspension, and some choices have a closing window whether you act or not.

So give it a real deadline. Not tomorrow, and not someday. I will decide by the end of the month. Put it on the calendar. A deadline turns an endless dread into a process with a shape.

Separate the fear of choosing from the fear of the outcome

Two different fears get mixed together here. One is fear of the thing itself: the surgery, the divorce, the move. The other is fear of being the person who chose it, of having no one else to blame if it goes badly.

The second fear is what makes people freeze, delay until the choice is made for them, or push someone else into deciding. Notice it if it is there, and notice that it is not information about which option is better. It is just fear of responsibility, and it does not go away by waiting; waiting is also a choice you will own.

Get the facts that can be gotten

Before you get to values, get the information that actually exists. For a medical decision, that means asking your doctor the specific questions: what happens if I do nothing, what are the real risks, what does recovery look like, how many of these have you done, what would you tell someone in my situation. A second opinion is normal and rarely resented.

For other decisions, it means talking to people who have made the same one, especially several of them, and especially those for whom it did not go perfectly. You are trying to replace imagination with something closer to reality.

Then decide by values, not by outcome

Here is the hard truth about permanent decisions: you cannot know how they turn out. Any certainty you manufacture will be false. So the question cannot be which one works out. It has to be which one fits what matters to me.

Ask yourself what you actually value most in this part of your life. Safety or aliveness. Loyalty or honesty. Being close to family or being free. Then look at the options and ask which one is more true to that. A decision made from your values can be lived with even when it turns out badly, because you know why you made it. A decision made from a guess about outcomes leaves you with nothing to stand on when the guess is wrong.

Try each one on for a few days

Instead of weighing endlessly, decide privately that the answer is yes and live with it for three days. Notice what happens in your body and what you find yourself thinking. Then decide it is no and live with that for three days. People often find far more clarity in this than in another round of lists. Relief and dread are data.

Talk to one or two people, not everyone

Crowdsourcing a permanent decision is a way to get more confused and more influenced by whoever spoke last. Pick one or two people who know you well, will not push their own agenda, and can tolerate hearing you think out loud without rushing you. Ask them to reflect back what they hear rather than to tell you what to do.

Accept that grief comes either way

Every real choice closes a door, and permanent choices close it for good. Even the right decision will come with loss: the life you would have had, the version of yourself that would have existed. Expect to grieve that, even when you are certain. Grief alongside a decision is not evidence that the decision was wrong.

Decide once, and then stop relitigating

Once you choose, close it. Write down what you decided and why, in your own words, while the reasons are fresh. On the hard days afterward, read that instead of reopening the whole question. You will have hard days. They are not a verdict.

When you cannot think at all

If the weight of the decision has you unable to sleep, function, or think straight, or if you are being pressured or rushed by someone with a stake in the answer, please talk to a therapist or counselor before deciding. A neutral person whose only job is to help you think can make an enormous difference, and they will not push you toward an answer. For medical choices, many hospitals also have patient advocates or counselors who do exactly this. A decision this size deserves support, and you do not have to carry it alone.', array['Decision Making', 'Values']::text[], 0, '2026-09-20T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('when-work-feels-meaningless', 'When Work Feels Meaningless', 'The job is fine. The pay is fine. And you sit down each morning wondering what the point is. What that flatness usually means, and what to do about it short of quitting.', 'Nothing is wrong, exactly. The work is not too hard. Your manager is fine. You are not in crisis. You just find yourself staring at the screen wondering why any of it matters, moving tasks from one column to another, watching the clock in a way you did not used to.

This is one of the most common quiet complaints at work, and it rarely gets said out loud, because it sounds ungrateful. Let us take it seriously, because it usually means something specific, and the specifics point to different fixes.

Meaninglessness usually has a cause

The flat feeling is not random. Look through this list and see which one lands.

You cannot see the effect of your work. Humans need to see that what they do lands somewhere. When your piece disappears into a process and you never learn what happened to it, motivation drains regardless of the pay.

Nothing you do seems to change the outcome. When the same decisions get made whatever you recommend, when good work and poor work are treated identically, effort stops feeling connected to anything.

The work conflicts with what you value. This one is heavier. If the job asks you to sell something you do not believe in, cut corners you think matter, or be someone you do not respect, the flatness is a moral signal rather than a motivational one.

You have outgrown it. You mastered this a while ago. There is nothing left to learn, and mastery without challenge becomes boredom, which feels a lot like meaninglessness.

You are depleted. Sometimes the work has not changed and you have. Exhaustion makes everything look pointless, and no amount of purpose will fix a sleep deficit or a stretch of grief.

Start with the last one

Before you rethink your career, check whether you are simply worn out. How is your sleep? When did you last take time off that was actually restful? Has something difficult been happening in your life outside work? Meaninglessness is one of the most reliable symptoms of burnout and of low mood, and both of them lie convincingly about the cause.

If this is it, the fix is rest and support, not a new job. People who quit at this point often find the flatness waiting for them in the next role.

Go find the effect of your work

If you cannot see where your work lands, go looking. Ask to see the customer feedback. Ask the team downstream what they do with what you send. Ask to sit in on the meeting where it gets used. Talk to one person who benefits from the thing you make.

This sounds small and it is surprisingly powerful. Most people''s sense of purpose at work comes from a concrete line between their effort and another person, and that line is often just hidden rather than absent.

Change the shape of the work before changing the job

Most jobs have more flexibility than people use. Ask for a project in the part you find interesting. Offer to mentor someone new, which reliably restores meaning for people who have outgrown the tasks. Take on the annoying problem nobody has fixed. Learn the skill that would let you do a different piece of the work.

A conversation with your manager about what you want more of is a normal conversation, and managers generally prefer it to losing someone.

Stop expecting the job to carry all of it

Some work is just work. It pays for a life, and the meaning lives in the life. That is not a failure or a compromise; it has been true for most people in most of history. If your job is decent and not harmful, it is allowed to be the thing that funds the parts of your life that matter, and building those parts up deliberately, the people, the projects, the community, can settle the restlessness better than another job hunt.

Pay attention if it is a values conflict

The one version of this that does not resolve with tweaks is when the work asks you to act against what you believe. That erodes people steadily and shows up as cynicism, shame, and a flatness that follows you home. If that is your situation, the question is not how to feel better about it. It is what your exit looks like, and it is worth planning carefully rather than dramatically.

Give it a real experiment

Pick one change, whether a conversation, a new project, a protected week of rest, and give it a month. Then check in with yourself honestly. If the flatness lifts, you learned something. If it does not, you have better information for a bigger decision, and you made it deliberately rather than from exhaustion.

When it is more than the job

If the emptiness is not only at work, if nothing anywhere interests you, if you are sleeping badly, withdrawing from people, or feeling hopeless, that is worth talking to a doctor or therapist about rather than treating as a career problem. Low mood often arrives first as a loss of meaning, and it is very treatable. Either way, you deserve to spend your days on something that feels like it counts, and figuring out what that is is a reasonable thing to want help with.', array['Work', 'Values']::text[], 0, '2026-09-20T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz)
